<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kobayakawa clan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kobayakawa were a Japanese warrior clan of Aki province in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>. In the 16th century Mōri Motonari placed his son Takakage at its head, and with the Kikkawa it became one of the 'two rivers' supporting the Mōri. Takakage served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a> as one of his senior regents, and his adopted heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Kobayakawa Hideaki</a> decided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a> in 1600 by changing sides; the line ended with his death in 1602.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-clan.jpg" alt="Kobayakawa clan" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: BraneJ · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hidari_mitsudomoe.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kobayakawa claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-clan">Taira clan</a>, through its Kanmu-Heishi branch, which traced itself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-kanmu">Emperor Kanmu</a>, the 50th emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Nakamura Munehira, son of Taira no Tsunemune, founded the Nakamura clan of Sagami Province. His second son, Dohi Sanehira, who died in 1191, founded the Dohi clan, and Sanehira's second son, Kobayakawa Tohira, who died in 1237, founded the Kobayakawa. Two sons of the third head of the family, Kobayakawa Shigehira, who died in 1264, divided the clan: Masahira, his third son, made his base at Numata and began the Numata-Kobayakawa, and Masakage, his fourth son, settled at Takehara and began the Takehara-Kobayakawa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The two branches continued side by side for some three centuries; the lists of their heads end with the same man, Kobayakawa Takakage, born in 1533, the third son of Mōri Motonari. Takakage was the uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a>, and his brother headed the allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kikkawa-clan">Kikkawa clan</a>, so that the Kobayakawa held great power in the politics of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Toyotomi Hideyoshi regarded Takakage as one of the best statesmen and appointed him to the Council of Five Elders, but Takakage died in 1597, before Hideyoshi.</p><p>At the end of the 16th century the Kobayakawa fought beside the Kikkawa, Mōri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-clan">Ōtomo clans</a> against the Shimazu for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a>, and after the defeat of the Shimazu they were awarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chikuzen-province">Chikuzen Province</a> as their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. Takakage's successor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobayakawa-hideaki">Kobayakawa Hideaki</a>, born in 1582, was a nephew of Hideyoshi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>A powerful clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>, the Kobayakawa were disbanded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-era">Meiji period</a> the Mōri clan restored the house, which was given the title of baron in the new nobility. The Kusai clan of Takehara, a branch of the Kobayakawa, was also granted noble rank.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kobayakawa clan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Motonari placed his sons at the head of the Kikkawa and <b>Kobayakawa</b> families, the two rivers supporting the Mori, and legend tells of his lesson of three arrows on family unity.</blockquote><a href="/state/mori-clan-domain">Mori clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
